The Sinchew War was fought from 1648 to 1656 in [[Sinchew]], in [[Hachinawa]] and in the interior between them. It began as a rising of the Baiduist population of the colony against the Tianist colonial administration, and it became a war between the Zong colonies and Hachinawa in which the empire lost its western and southern territories.

Four parties fought in it. The Baiduist revolutionary army was raised by the [[Ocean Crossing Society]] and marched on [[Tamsui]] in 1648. The [[Sinchew Colony]] and the [[Kingdom of Zong]] defended the capital. The [[Louyang Colony]] sent its navy to the colony's assistance and its army into Hachinawa, with the object of taking the empire's territory for itself. Hachinawa entered the war on the colony's side in June 1648, and its king was driven from his capital.

The war ended in a settled peace rather than a victory. Hachinawa was partitioned between the colony, the empire's own royal house and Louyang, and about 50,000 Baiduists were executed or displaced. The remnant of the revolutionary army withdrew to the north-east and established [[Free Sinchew]] on land neither victor had claimed.

== Theatres ==

The war was fought on three fronts, and the parties on each changed during it.

The Sinchew front, in the colony's own territory, was the front on which the war began. The Baiduist revolutionary army operated on it from the reduction of the lake settlements in the spring of 1648, and the defence of [[Tamsui]] was fought there. After 1648 the front moved north and east as the army withdrew, and it was on this front that the army was reduced in 1655.

The Hachinawa front began as a frontier the colony watched rather than a place it fought. Hachinawa was the power whose intentions [[Ng Tek-seng]] had warned the court of, and until June 1648 it was the quarter from which the colony expected trouble. From 16 June, when the imperial army arrived in the colony's support, the front became one of alliance rather than of opposition. It became a front of a third kind in September 1648, when [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]]'s land force entered the empire and took [[Hachintala]].

The northern front was opened by the revolutionary army's withdrawal. Driven from the lake country, it retreated north and east through the interior and into territory that neither Hachinawa nor the colony had claimed, and it was there that [[Free Sinchew]] was established in the last years of the war.

== 1648 ==

The rising began at the lake. The Ocean Crossing Society, which had been founded among the Baiduist settlements of the interior in the 1620s as a religious and mutual-aid association, raised an armed force in 1648 and took the settlements along the shores of [[Tn̂g-ô͘]]. It was commanded by Ng Bú-seng (黃武生), a Baiduist who had served as a garrison commander in the [[Third Mahik War]], and it was financed by Baiduist merchant houses holding property in the colony. A force of about 25,000 was assembled, and its object was the overthrow of the colonial government at Tamsui.

=== Righteous March ===

The army left its base at [[Takau]] on the lake on 13 April 1648 and marched on Tamsui, which it reached on 18 May. The distance is about 218 km and the column covered it at rather more than six kilometres a day, which is slow for an army and consistent with a popular movement: the force marched with its families, its livestock and no supply train, and it foraged as it went.

The march is known as the Righteous March. It was the first occasion on which the Baiduist population of the colony acted as a single body, and the settlements it passed through supplied it as it advanced.

=== Defence of Tamsui ===

The city held. The First and Second Imperial Fleets of Zong, about 7,000 men between them under Admiral Lîm Chiau-hong, were lying in the bay when the army arrived, and with the colonial garrison of about 3,000 they held the walls and the harbour against it.

The outcome turned on the fleet's presence rather than on numbers. The revolutionary army was a risen militia; the fleets were regulars with naval artillery, and the fire from the bay could reach the approaches to the city. On 11 June the defenders drove the army back from Tamsui and it withdrew into the highlands.

=== Interventions ===

Hachinawa learned of the rising within the week and sent an army of about 50,000 men under General Hachatsi, which arrived on 16 June. The [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] administration learned of it about a month after the march began and despatched its entire naval force of about 16,000 under Admiral Tân Khéng-bûn, which arrived in July. Both came to the assistance of the colony.

Louyang sent a second force by land: about 8,000 men under General Ng Chì-seng, based in eastern Louyang, which advanced north through Hachinawa. Its instructions were not to assist the empire.

The governor of Louyang, Tân Chì-hong, sought the whole of Hachinawa for his own colony. He had concluded that the colony's forces would be engaged with the rising, which was correct, and that an opportunity had opened. He also held that the empire had forfeited any claim to consideration by its conduct in the war, and treated Hachinawa as a party that had gone over to the enemy. The force entered the empire, pillaged the farms on its route, and reached [[Hachintala]] on 15 September 1648.

The king of Hachinawa, [[Kayatsi]], left the capital before it fell, and was taken in at [[Tamsui]] by [[Ng Tek-seng]].

His presence in the city was concealed. Tamsui was held by the forces of Zong and Louyang as well as by the colony's own garrison, and Louyang was the power that had taken the king's capital. Kayatsi remained in the city through the war without his presence being declared, and no announcement of it was made while the war continued.

Kayatsi did not consent to the partition that ended it. He had left his capital and taken refuge with the colony rather than submit, and he argued throughout the war for the restoration of the empire's former territories. The settlement of 1656 divided Hachinawa without restoring it, and the king's agreement to it was obtained by the terms on which the remainder was assigned to his house. His son Talasho and the royal family went east to the empire's Aethiopean territory, which by 1648 had been enlarged by the [[Hachinawa–Jacano War]] of 1634 to 1637 and was governed as the province the empire called [[Etowah]].

=== King's reception ===

The reception of Kayatsi at Tamsui was arranged before the war. Ng Tek-seng had informed the Hachina court of Louyang's interest in invading the empire, and of the colony's own willingness to support Hachinawa against it. He had also told the court that the arrangement had a limit: if the colony itself came under threat, from the Baiduists or from Hachinawa, Louyang would have grounds for treating the colony as the aggressor and acting against it.

Kayatsi was accordingly taken into Tamsui and concealed there, in a city occupied by the forces of Zong and Louyang, and the arrangement held until the governor chose to disclose it. The governor's position throughout was that the colony's obligations to the empire and its dependence on Louyang had to be held at the same time, and the reception of the king was the arrangement by which both were maintained.

Louyang had its own account of the matter. Its administration claimed that Hachinawa had concerted with the Baiduists, and it relied on the fact that the commander of the revolutionary army was a Baiduist who had held a colonial commission in the Third Mahik War and had been in communication with Hachina houses before the rising.

== 1649–1655 ==

The war continued for seven years after the defence of Tamsui. The revolutionary army was driven from the lake country and withdrew north and east, and it was pursued rather than destroyed. It remained in being throughout, and its operations after 1648 were directed at the settlements nearest to it rather than at the cities.

Louyang retained the territory it had taken in Hachinawa and garrisoned it, and the forces of the colony and of Zong were occupied in the interior. Hachinawa's army of 50,000 was engaged against Louyang's force in the empire's own territory, and the empire's government was conducted from Tamsui.

The Baiduist population of the colony was divided by the war. Numbers of Baiduists turned against the revolutionary army in these years to avoid being treated as its supporters, and the colony's returns for the period record the movement of Baiduist households away from the districts the army operated in.

== 1656 ==

The war ended in a settled peace rather than the defeat of any party.

The settlement began with the king's appearance. The revolutionary army was reduced in the colony's own territory in the course of 1655, and with the rising no longer a danger to Tamsui, [[Ng Tek-seng]] brought Kayatsi out of concealment and into the negotiations. Until that point the king had been in the city without his presence being declared; he was produced as a party to the settlement when the governor had a settlement to propose.

The timing was the governor's. While the revolutionary army was in the field, the colony's need of Louyang and of the fleets was greater than its obligations to Hachinawa, and the disclosure of the king would have given Louyang the grounds it had been seeking. Once the rising was suppressed, the colony's position was stronger and Kayatsi could be produced as a sovereign whose territories were among the matters to be settled.

Hachinawa was partitioned. Its western territories north of the [[Qingjiang]] were merged into the Sinchew Colony, and were given semi-autonomous control under the colonial administration. Its eastern territories were assigned to the royal house, which was recognised as the successor state to Hachinawa. Its southern territories were merged into Louyang. The empire that had been the largest polity on the western coast in 1618 ceased to exist as a single state, and the portion assigned to the royal house is the territory from which the modern state of Sinchew descends.

About 50,000 Baiduists were executed or displaced in the settlement of the colony. The figure is of the same order as the population of the lake settlements in 1645, and the displacement was concentrated in those districts.

The revolutionary army withdrew to the north-east. In the last years of the war it was in retreat and foraging, and it left the settled country altogether for land that neither Hachinawa nor the colony had claimed. It established [[Free Sinchew]] there, and held it until 1715, when it was conquered by the Sinchew Colony in the [[First War of the Isthmus]].

== Aftermath ==

The war removed Hachinawa as a power on the western coast and made the colony the largest polity in the region. The Sinchew Colony took the empire's western territory and Louyang took its southern, and the boundary between the two colonies was again drawn along the [[Qingjiang]].

The Baiduist population of the colony, which had been the majority since 1610, was reduced by the executions and displacements of 1656 and by the departure of those who joined the revolutionary army. The Tianist minority retained the offices and the land.

